<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_set.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-set">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-set">
  <primary>SET</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>SET</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>SET</refname>
  <refpurpose>change the properties of bound vertices and edges</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
SET <replaceable class="parameter">assignment</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">assignment</replaceable> ] ...

where <replaceable class="parameter">assignment</replaceable> is one of:

    <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable>.<replaceable class="parameter">key</replaceable> [ .<replaceable class="parameter">key</replaceable> ] ... = <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable>
    <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> = <replaceable class="parameter">map</replaceable>
    <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> = <replaceable class="parameter">element</replaceable>
    <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> += <replaceable class="parameter">map</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-set-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>SET</command> writes properties onto elements an earlier clause has bound. What
   happens depends on the left side and on the operator.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>n.key = expression</literal> sets one property, adding the key if it is not there.
   <literal>n.key = NULL</literal> removes that key instead of storing a null.
   <xref linkend="cypher-remove"/> says the same thing more plainly.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>n = <replaceable class="parameter">map</replaceable></literal> replaces the whole
   property bag, so keys the map does not list are lost, and
   <literal>n = <replaceable class="parameter">element</replaceable></literal> replaces it with
   the bag of another vertex or edge. <literal>n += <replaceable class="parameter">map</replaceable></literal>
   merges the map into the bag: the listed keys are set and the rest are kept.
   <literal>=</literal> is a replacement and <literal>+=</literal> is an update, which is the
   difference to be careful about. A key needs no quoting, inside a map or after a dot. Quote
   one in single quotes when it is not a plain name, as in <literal>m.'release date'</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Assignments in one clause are separated by commas and applied left to right.
   <command>SET</command> runs once per incoming row, so a <command>MATCH</command> that finds
   ten vertices updates ten of them. It needs a bound variable and cannot introduce one, so it
   cannot lead a query. It may end one. Add a <xref linkend="cypher-return"/> to see the
   result.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>SET</command> writes properties only. It cannot add or change a label.
   <literal>ON CREATE SET</literal> and <literal>ON MATCH SET</literal> on a
   <xref linkend="cypher-merge"/> take this same assignment list.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-set-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A vertex or edge variable already in scope. Bare, it replaces or merges the whole bag.
      Followed by one or more keys, it addresses a single property, nested as deep as the keys
      go.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable></term>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">map</replaceable></term>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">element</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The value to store. Against a property it is any Cypher expression: a literal, a
      property of another element, an arithmetic or <literal>CASE</literal> expression, a list
      or a map. For <literal>+=</literal>, and for <literal>=</literal> against a bare
      variable, it has to be a map or another graph element.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-set-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Add a film to work on:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:movie {title: 'Tenet', year: 2020, genre: 'Action'});

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
   Set one property. <literal>properties()</literal> returns the whole bag, which is the
   easiest way to see what a <command>SET</command> did:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
SET m.runtime = 150
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

                                props
---------------------------------------------------------------------
 {"year": 2020, "genre": "Action", "title": "Tenet", "runtime": 150}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Several properties in one clause:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
SET m.genre = 'Sci-Fi', m.runtime = 151
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

                                props
---------------------------------------------------------------------
 {"year": 2020, "genre": "Sci-Fi", "title": "Tenet", "runtime": 151}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Assigning null deletes the key rather than storing a null:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
SET m.runtime = NULL
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

                        props
-----------------------------------------------------
 {"year": 2020, "genre": "Sci-Fi", "title": "Tenet"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>+=</literal> sets the keys the map lists and leaves the others alone:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
SET m += {runtime: 150, imax: true}
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

                                       props
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 {"imax": true, "year": 2020, "genre": "Sci-Fi", "title": "Tenet", "runtime": 150}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>=</literal> against a bare variable throws away everything not in the map.
   <literal>genre</literal>, <literal>runtime</literal> and <literal>imax</literal> are gone:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
SET m = {title: 'Tenet', year: 2020}
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

              props
----------------------------------
 {"year": 2020, "title": "Tenet"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The right side can be another element, which copies its whole bag:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:person {name: 'Stand-in'});

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Stand-in'}), (k:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})
SET p = k
RETURN properties(p) AS props;

                 props
----------------------------------------
 {"born": 1964, "name": "Keanu Reeves"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Copying a bag copies the identifying properties too, so there are now two vertices whose
   <literal>name</literal> is <literal>Keanu Reeves</literal>. It does not copy edges, and it
   does not merge the two vertices.
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge takes properties exactly as a vertex does. This <command>MATCH</command> finds the
   one edge that belongs to the original Keanu Reeves, because the copy has no edges:
<programlisting>
MATCH (:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
SET r.credited = true
RETURN properties(r) AS props;

               props
-----------------------------------
 {"role": "Neo", "credited": true}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An empty map removes every property and leaves the element in place. The vertex is then
   unreachable by any property:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
SET m = {}
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

 props
-------
 {}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-set-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   The default setting <varname>allow_null_properties</varname> is off, which is what makes
   <literal>SET n.key = NULL</literal> a deletion. Turn it on and the key is kept with a JSON
   null value instead:
<programlisting>
SET allow_null_properties = true;
CREATE (m:movie {title: 'Tenet', year: 2020})
SET m.runtime = NULL
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

SET
                       props
---------------------------------------------------
 {"year": 2020, "title": "Tenet", "runtime": null}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Set it back to off when you are done. A stored null is not the same as an absent key.
   <literal>m.runtime IS NULL</literal> is true either way, but the key shows up in
   <literal>properties()</literal> and in a <type>jsonb</type> key test.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-set-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>SET</command> implements the property assignments of the GQL set statement,
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 section 13.3. The standard's set label item, which adds a label to an
   element, is not accepted in either spelling, <literal>SET n:blockbuster</literal> or
   <literal>SET n IS blockbuster</literal>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Inception'}) SET m:blockbuster RETURN m.title;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near ":"
LINE 1: MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Inception'}) SET m:blockbuster RETUR...
                                                  ^
</programlisting>
   A label is the table an element lives in, so it is fixed when the element is created. To
   move a vertex to another label, create it there and delete the original.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher uses the same assignment forms, and AgensGraph accepts them unchanged. Its label
   form <literal>SET n:Label</literal> is refused the same way.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

